`{ recursive: true }` is not supported by Node.js `fs.watch` on Linux (only macOS and Windows). On Linux this throws `ERR_FEATURE_UNAVAILABLE_ON_PLATFORM`, which the surrounding `try/catch` silently swallows and logs at debug level. The result is that file watching silently does nothing on Linux CI and Linux dev servers, so changed components are never re-extracted until the next full page reload.

Consider a cross-platform alternative: use the `chokidar` package (already a transitive dependency in many Storybook configurations) or `@parcel/watcher`, both of which fall back to polling on Linux. Alternatively, detect the platform and emit a warning when recursive watching is unavailable:
```ts
if (process.platform === 'linux') {
  logger.warn('[reactComponentMeta] Recursive fs.watch unsupported on Linux; file watching disabled.');
  return;
}
```

`ComponentMetaManager.instance` is a static field that is assigned once and never cleared. If `dispose()` is ever called outside the `process.on('exit', ...)` handler (e.g. during a plugin-level restart or in a future test that uses `getInstance()`), subsequent calls to `getInstance()` will return a Promise resolving to the already-disposed manager — causing `ls.getProgram()` calls on a disposed LanguageService.

A minimal fix is to reset the field in `dispose()` so that the next caller gets a fresh instance:
```ts
static async disposeInstance() {
  const m = await this.instance;
  this.instance = undefined;
  m?.dispose();
}
```
Or at minimum document that `getInstance()` must not be called after `dispose()`.

The production source files (`ComponentMetaManager.ts`, `ComponentMetaProject.ts`) import Node built-ins without the `node:` protocol prefix (`'fs'`, `'path'`), while all the new test files consistently use `'node:path'` and `'node:fs'`. The rest of the codebase uses `node:` for built-ins. Please align the production imports:
```ts
import { type FSWatcher, existsSync, watch } from 'node:fs';
import * as path from 'node:path';
```

`commandLine.fileNames.includes(fileName)` is an O(n) scan inside a `for` loop, making `ensureFiles(fileNames)` O(n²) in the number of file names. For a large monorepo project (e.g. Mantine with 133 components each pulling in many story files), this will be called with a large `allFiles` array on every batch extract.

Consider maintaining a `Set<string>` mirror of `commandLine.fileNames`, or at minimum build a `Set` once at the top of `ensureFiles`:
```ts
ensureFiles(fileNames: string[]): void {
  const existing = new Set(this.commandLine.fileNames);
  const toAdd = fileNames.filter(f => !existing.has(f));
  if (toAdd.length > 0) {
    this.commandLine.fileNames.push(...toAdd);
    this.projectVersion++;
  }
}
```

`this.entries = entries` overwrites the stored entries on every call to `extractPropsFromStories`. The warmup timer that fires 100 ms after a file change re-extracts `this.entries`, which only contains the *last* batch — not the union of all batches requested since the project was created. In a dev server handling multiple simultaneous manifest requests (e.g. different story groups), some components may never be warmed up after a file change.

Consider accumulating entries or documenting the intentional last-wins behaviour so future maintainers understand why the warmup is best-effort:
```ts
// Only the most-recently-extracted batch is re-warmed; this is intentional.
// Full correctness is ensured by on-demand extraction; warmup only speeds up the common path.
this.entries = entries;
```

`arrayItemsEqual` builds `new Set(a)` and then checks membership for every element of `b`. This makes the comparison order-insensitive (correct for file lists) but also insensitive to duplicate counts: if `a = ['x', 'y']` and `b = ['x', 'x']`, the lengths match (2 === 2), every element of `b` is found in the set, and the function returns `true` — incorrectly reporting the arrays as equal.

In practice TypeScript file lists should not contain duplicates, so the bug is unlikely to trigger, but the function should either document this assumption or be fixed:
```ts
// Correct O(n) set-based comparison (assumes no duplicates in either array)
function arrayItemsEqual(a: string[], b: string[]) {
  if (a.length !== b.length) return false;
  const setA = new Set(a);
  return b.every(f => setA.has(f));
  // If duplicates are possible, use a frequency map instead.
}
```
